 Google Workspace service-account credentials (DWD) are **not** in `.env` — they live encrypted in the database, managed via `GoogleWorkspaceConfigService` (Settings UI).
 
 `WA_DB_SPECS=1` is **dev-only**, not a production variable — it is set permanently for the backend service in the dev `docker-compose.yml` so every container `jest` run exercises the WhatsApp DB-backed specs (`test/whatsapp`); without it those specs skip with one warn line (no DB probe, no hang) and the host run is the unit-level merge gate only. It has no meaning in `docker-compose.vps.yml` / prod.
 
 `WA_GRAPH_BASE_URL` is **dev-only too, and must stay unset in production.** It overrides the Meta Graph base URL — `wa-graph.client.ts` is the only reader (pinned by a guard-cell spec that fails if a second file ever reads it, and that fails if the production default literal ever changes), trailing slashes stripped, so a local stack can point every campaign send at a mock Graph server for the WP5 verification loop (`docs/archive/whatsapp-module/WP5-PLAN.md` Task V) instead of a real one. `docker-compose.yml` passes it through empty by default. When it IS set, the client logs one WARN at construction naming the override — deliberately loud, because a production container with this set would send every message into whatever answers at that URL and never reach a phone. The deploy env inventory lists it as **unset**; it is not part of the `WhatsApp native` row above because that row is production-relevant variables, and this one by design is never one.
 
